WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.280
Weak AI-assisted results often begin with an unclear request.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.940
"Build an app" leaves the assistant to guess the user, the environment, the data, the interface,

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.760
and the standard for success.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:18.220
A stronger request explains who the software is for, what outcome it should produce, what inputs it receives,

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.040
what outputs it creates, and what must remain unchanged.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.780
Before asking for code, define the smallest useful outcome.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.600
"Improve reporting" is a direction, not an implementation target.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.660
"Add comma-separated value export to the existing report page without changing the database schema" gives the

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.120
assistant a narrower problem to solve.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.860
Small outcomes make the first patch easier to review and easier to reject if it heads in the wrong direction.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.360
Constraints are part of the task, not optional decoration.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.900
The assistant needs to know the programming language and version, the framework already in use,

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:58.920
supported operating systems, browser expectations, data formats, accessibility needs, performance limits,

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.500
security requirements, and files that should not be touched.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.360
If new libraries are not allowed, say so.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.620
If the command must run on Windows and Linux, say that too.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.000
Success criteria should be observable.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.220
"Make it better" is hard to test.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:20.960
"The command accepts an input file, rejects malformed rows, and writes a valid JavaScript Object Notation result" can

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.140
be checked.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.320
These criteria do not need to become heavy paperwork.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.260
A few precise acceptance criteria give both the developer and the assistant a shared target.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.360
They also make it easier to notice when an attractive implementation solves a different problem.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:41.020
When the task is ambiguous or consequential, ask the assistant to restate the plan before it writes code.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:46.840
That pause can reveal a wrong assumption about the data model, the user interface, or the files to edit.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.940
Clear intent does not require a perfect specification.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:56.080
It reduces avoidable ambiguity enough to make the first implementation useful, bounded, and reviewable.
